\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Pinned affine massless laws}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let a finite nonempty graph be connected, with positive edge weights.
Put #{n=|V|} and fix a vertex #{p}. Its massless action defines a probability law on
#{F_p=\{\phi:\phi(p)=0\}} with induced counting volume.
For any #{b\in\mathbb R}, translation by #{b\mathbf1_V} defines the
law on the affine constraint #{\phi(p)=b}; its mean is
#{b\mathbf1_V} and its covariance is that of the zero-pinned law.}
\proof{
\p{Write #{i_F:F_p\to\mathbb R^V} for inclusion and
#{p_F:\mathbb R^V\to F_p} for orthogonal projection. The compressed
precision is
##{Q_F=p_F L i_F:F_p\to F_p.}
It is self-adjoint, since
#{h_F(x,Q_Fy)=h_V(i_Fx,Li_Fy)}. A zero-energy vector is constant by
the [kernel theorem](fdip-0008); vanishing at #{p} makes it zero.
Thus #{Q_F} is positive definite. [Normalization](fdip-002T) on the
inner-product space #{F_p} gives
##{Z_F=(2\pi)^{(n-1)/2}/\sqrt{\det Q_F}.}
The centered law has covariance #{Q_F^{-1}} on #{F_p}, and ambient
covariance #{i_F Q_F^{-1}p_F}, by \ref{fdip-000B}.
The subspace need not be invariant under #{L}; the precision is the
compressed quadratic form, not an endomorphism restriction of #{L}.}
\p{The affine constraint equals #{b\mathbf1_V+F_p}. Define its
induced volume by translating the volume on #{F_p}. Since
#{D(b\mathbf1_V+\psi)=D\psi}, this translation preserves the action
and normalizer. Centeredness on #{F_p} gives the stated mean;
subtracting that mean gives exactly the zero-pinned covariance.
For a single vertex #{F_p=0}, the normalizer is one and the law is
concentrated at the value #{b}.}
}
\p{These are laws on their own constraint spaces. They are not defined
by conditioning on an event in an unconstrained massless probability,
whose normalizer diverges. Fixed external boundary values also remove
zero modes in finite lattice regions, as in
\citet{Definition 8.1, Remark 8.2 and Exercise 8.2, p. 381}{friedli2017statistical}.
That source's lattice boundary and volume conventions remain part of its
model; the induced-volume construction here proves the finite-graph claim.}
